<!--Post: 8219--><!--Time: 1109006737-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST8219"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Tecno242<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Tecno242<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->2-2005<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Monday, February 21, 2005 - 9:25 am: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->I did quite a bit of research into this and found a number of possibilities.  Unfortunately, I think the most likely of these is also the most obvious and simple, but I will lay out everything I found, there could be a connection buried somewhere in here.  First i would like to explain though that searching X Factor as compared to searching THE X Factor returned different things, but I will post both.  Here they are; <BR> <BR>1.  The X Factor, a book by Andre Norton, Science Fiction, published in 1965  The intresting part of this book is that the &#34;hero&#34; of the book is and I quote &#34;a mutant son of a high-ranking military official&#34;  I thought this was intresting because of the speculation that BTK is the son of a military man. &#42; someone should find this book in the Wichita library just to make sure nothing is planted inside, i would also like to know the names of the chapters.   <BR> <BR>2. Factor X, a rare blood disorder, occurs in 1 in 500,000.  Known for at least 30 years.  Similar to a hemophiliac.  Also called the Stuart-Prower deficiency.   <BR> <BR>3. Factor X, marvel comics, first issue was 1963.  I read through the names of all published Factor X comics and didn&#39;t notice any intresting connections although there could be some inside the actual comic.   <BR> <BR>4. Factor X, known to horse racing as the connection between a horses heart and track performance.  But also can just mean the genetic material inherited from the female chromosone.  Could it somehow be a reference to his mother? <BR> <BR>5. The X Factor, is simply defined as &#34;the effect of unknowable circumstances on any given situation&#34; <BR> <BR>6. I&#39;ve read from other posts that X Factor is also some type of complex engineering mathmatics, but I could not find anything on this. <BR> <BR>7. The simpliest and I think most likely, the shortest definition of Factor X is simply &#34;The Unknown&#34; in which he is most likely referring to himself.  He IS Factor X.  <!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 8219-->
<!--Post: 8253--><!--Time: 1109015372-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#cccccc"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST8253"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Joel<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Joel<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->2-2005<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Monday, February 21, 2005 - 11:49 am: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->&#39;3. Factor X, marvel comics, first issue was 1963.&#39; <BR> <BR>The comic was called &#39;X-Men&#39;, and although it features a concept called Factor X this was not a title of a comic featuring the X-Men &#40;although a later 80&#39;s title was called &#39;X-Factor&#39;.&#41; <BR> <BR>Nice research though, I hadn&#39;t heard of some of these.<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 8253-->
<!--Post: 8256--><!--Time: 1109017270-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST8256"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Nm_pi<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Nm_pi<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->2-2005<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Monday, February 21, 2005 - 12:21 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text--><a href="http://www.copi.com/articles/group13.html" target=_top>http://www.copi.com/articles/group13.html</a><!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 8256-->
<!--Post: 8614--><!--Time: 1109248165-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#cccccc"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST8614"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Birdieict<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Birdieict<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->1-2005<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Thursday, February 24, 2005 - 4:29 am: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->An idea stemming from the Blackout thread...Factor X is ecstasy tablets? The killings are the effect of drugs?<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 8614-->
<!--Post: 8733--><!--Time: 1109301395-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST8733"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><a href="mailto:bmodule_244@hotmail.com" target="_blank"><!--/email--><!--name-->Bmodule<!--/name--></b></a>
</a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Bmodule<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->2-2005<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Thursday, February 24, 2005 - 7:16 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Not ecstacy. It&#39;s supposed to be a warm fuzzy drug. if it is drugs it&#39;s probably meth which over time is supposed to cause fits of rage and meglomania. Adolf Hitler&#39;s doctor was giving him a an injection of a cocktail made primarily of meth and something to keep him from farting.<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 8733-->
<!--Post: 8741--><!--Time: 1109303113-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#cccccc"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST8741"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Rocky<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Rocky<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->2-2005<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Thursday, February 24, 2005 - 7:45 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->B: there is only one other time I&#39;ve heard the word meglomaniac and that was in a political science class. How funny! Nevertheless, I believe the X-factor is a mind altering experience whether influenced by drugs, or as Tecno242 puts it a brainwashing experience. Regardless, there is a malfunction whether altered by choice or not! So, is it something innate that causes a malfunction or drugs? <!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 8741-->
<!--Post: 8783--><!--Time: 1109340986-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST8783"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Bluepup<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Bluepup<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->2-2005<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Friday, February 25, 2005 - 6:16 am: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->I think its more likely that he was referring to genetics as an &#34;excuse&#34; for his actions. If you google &#34;factor x, criminal gene&#34; you get many links to scientific and medical research articles discussing whether there is a &#34;criminal gene&#34; on the X chromosone. Some refer to this as Factor X. There is controversy about whether inheriting this factor is enough to cause criminal agression or if environmental factors also come into play.<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 8783-->
<!--Post: 8785--><!--Time: 1109342018-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#cccccc"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST8785"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Tecno242<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Tecno242<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->2-2005<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Friday, February 25, 2005 - 6:33 am: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->It&#39;s certainly possible that in using the term &#34;Factor X&#34; he was describing the underlying cause of the &#34;Monster&#34; that hides in his mind.  In which he may not know the true cause.   <BR> <BR>It is also possible that &#34;Factor X&#34; is either he himself or something in the case, that has yet to be found, that links him to these killings. <BR> <BR>It&#39;s hard for me to agree that he was referring to genetics as an &#34;excuse&#34; for his actions, simply because he has never showed remorse or any sign of a guilty conscience in his communications.  If he did, he would not have lasted 30 years without either commiting suicide or giving himself up.  He does not make excuses for his actions, he displays them as trophies.<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 8785-->
<!--Post: 8869--><!--Time: 1109369495-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST8869"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Bluepup<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Bluepup<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->2-2005<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Friday, February 25, 2005 - 2:11 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Tecno -- I beg to differ--and think you better read that letter again -- he says &#40;all in the same letter&#41; &#34;I apologize to society&#34; and then goes on to try to explain the monster in him and then near the end is where he mentions factor x.  I never said he showed remorse !!  But it sounds to me as if he thinks this &#34;factor x&#34; justifies him killing because he cannot control it. and obviously if someone inherits a &#34;factor x&#34; criminal gene, that is out of their control. I really think he believes that. &#40;I disagree, by the way, and think that environment, etc. also plays a role in peoples behavior.&#41; <BR><!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 8869-->
<!--Post: 9497--><!--Time: 1109539291-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#cccccc"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST9497"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Ozdude<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Ozdude<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->12-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Sunday, February 27, 2005 - 1:21 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->I remember an episode of I Dream of Jeanine where Maj. Nelson lied to Dr. Bellows about creating a Factor X compound resulting in much hilarity..Maj. Nelson was in the Air Force..but why was Maj. Healey&#39;s uniform a different color..that is the real mystery.. <BR><!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 9497-->
<!--Post: 10207--><!--Time: 1109814834-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST10207"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Rocky<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Rocky<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->2-2005<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Wednesday, March 02, 2005 - 5:53 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Oz - Go to General Folder and look at &#34;anyone still getting freaky emails&#34;. There is an individual on there that can explain it quite well, according to them.<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 10207-->
<!--Post: 11126--><!--Time: 1110449755-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#cccccc"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST11126"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Bestbud<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Bestbud<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->12-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Thursday, March 10, 2005 - 2:15 am: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->KEY CODES  <BR> <BR>You will find the following abbreviations, acronyms and codes in Air Force brochures:  <BR> <BR>NCV--Normal color vision <BR>NDP--Normal depth perception <BR>TS--Top Secret clearance <BR>X--Indicates X factor, the amount of weight an individual must be able to lift to qualify for a particular career field.  <BR>ASVAB--Armed Services Vocational Aptitude Battery Test. Each job has minimum requirements in Mechanical, Administrative, General and/or Electronics areas. <BR>NPS--Non-Prior Service  <BR>PS--Prior Service  <BR>IADT--Initial Active Duty for Training  <BR><!--/Text--></td></tr>
